        'en' => [
            'title' => 'In kidney failure the risk of invasive pneumococcal disease is 3.7-fold. After PCV15, PPSV23 follows at eight weeks.',
            'image_alt' => 'A translucent purple kidney with pale vessels, surrounded by broken teal arcs. Three dark-red paired cocci sit to the right.',
            'excerpt' => 'On 20 October 2026 two texts on pneumococcus in kidney disease appeared on Nefro-projekt. ACIP cites a 3.7-fold risk of invasive disease in kidney failure, and an eight-week interval from PCV15 to PPSV23. The schedules are on that site. Here is the classification error that stretches the interval to a year.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On 20 October 2026 I published two texts on pneumococcal vaccination on the Nefro-projekt site. The clinical review is <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=pneumokokove-ockovanie-ckd-dialyza"><em>Očkovanie proti pneumokokom pri chronickej chorobe obličiek a na dialýze</em></a>: vaccination against pneumococcus in chronic kidney disease and on dialysis. The version for patients is <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=pneumokoky-ockovanie-pre-pacientov"><em>Očkovanie proti pneumokokom, keď máte chorobu obličiek alebo chodíte na dialýzu</em></a>. The tables, schedules, and source links are there. Here is one classification error that, in a kidney patient, lengthens the gap in cover by almost a year.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr7203a1">2023 ACIP recommendation</a> cites a Kaiser Permanente Northern California cohort. Adults with stage 3 or 4 chronic kidney disease, a glomerular filtration rate of 15 to 59&nbsp;ml/min, had 1.2 times the risk of invasive pneumococcal disease compared with health-plan members overall. In kidney failure, filtration below 15&nbsp;ml/min or dialysis, the risk was 3.7 times. The same recommendation recalls that KDIGO 2012 recommends pneumococcal vaccination for adults with stages 4 and 5. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ckj/sfv030">Vandecasteele and colleagues</a>, in the Clinical Kidney Journal (2015), add that the response to the polysaccharide vaccine PPSV23 is weaker in chronic kidney disease and that antibody levels fall quickly. On dialysis, a second mechanism joins the impaired adaptive immunity: the procedure itself removes part of the serum antibodies. A weaker response is a reason to vaccinate earlier, in the predialysis period.</p>
<p>On 23 October 2024 ACIP moved the age from which one dose of a conjugate vaccine is recommended for every adult who has not had a PCV, from 65 to 50 (<a href="https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7401a1">Kobayashi and colleagues</a>, MMWR 2025). The recommendations for adults aged 19 to 49 with a risk condition did not move with that change. A patient with kidney failure in their thirties does not wait until fifty.</p>
<p>ACIP splits risk conditions into chronic diseases and immunocompromising conditions. Chronic renal failure and nephrotic syndrome are on the immunocompromising list. The recommendation says they might not otherwise be considered immunocompromising. They are listed because the schedule is the same. After PCV15, PPSV23 follows at a minimum of eight weeks in an immunocompromising condition, a cerebrospinal fluid leak, or a cochlear implant. In chronic disease without those conditions the minimum interval is one year. Eight weeks is meant to shorten the period without cover against serotypes that are only in PPSV23. Classifying a kidney patient with ordinary chronic disease leaves them in that gap for almost a year longer.</p>
<p>Two rules run across the 2023 schedules for an adult aged 19 to 64 with kidney failure or nephrotic syndrome. Someone who has received only PPSV23 adds a conjugate vaccine at least one year after the last dose. Someone whose first dose, with no earlier vaccination, is PCV20 or PCV21 does not then receive PPSV23. The series is complete. An unknown history is treated as no vaccination. The branches that start from PCV13 are in the table on the site.</p>
<p>In Slovakia the rule is <a href="https://www.slov-lex.sk/pravne-predpisy/SK/ZZ/2008/585/">Decree of the Ministry of Health No. 585/2008 Coll.</a>, section 9(2), as amended by Decree No. 128/2025 Coll., in force from 1 June 2025. If a physician decides on vaccination against invasive pneumococcal infection, it applies to people under specialist follow-up, regardless of age, with severe chronic disease of the airways, the heart and vessels, or with metabolic, renal, or immune disorders, and to children with the diseases listed in Annex 3. Annex 3 names, among those, nephrotic syndrome (N04) and kidney failure (N18–N19). The phrase “regardless of age” sits in the clause on people under follow-up for a renal disorder. The codes in the annex are tied to children. An adult with kidney failure falls under the general clause on renal disorders, not under the children’s annex. Availability and reimbursement of products change. Before an indication, the categorisation list and the summary of product characteristics have to be checked. The American schedules are more detailed. An indication in Slovakia is judged under the Slovak rule.</p>
<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.5527/wjn.v15.i1.114748">Puri and colleagues</a>, in a prospective cross-sectional study from northern India (World Journal of Nephrology, 2026), followed 132 adults starting haemodialysis. Mean age was 52.6 years, and 72.7% were men. Both a pneumococcal and an influenza vaccine had been given to 37 patients (28.0%). Hepatitis B vaccination was completed by 63 (47.7%). None of these three vaccines had been given to 73 (55.3%). The strongest predictor of vaccination was a prior nephrology consultation: a pneumococcal vaccine had been given to 47.1% of patients who had one and to 7.8% of those who had not (p &lt; 0.001). One centre, in another system. I do not carry the absolute shares over to Slovakia. The direction, yes. Without predialysis care that has vaccination among its tasks, most people remain unvaccinated.</p>
<p>The 2015 review says where the evidence stops. A protective antibody level after vaccination in chronic kidney disease is not reliably established. Data on how much vaccination reduces morbidity and mortality in this population are missing. In people on dialysis there is an epidemiological association between vaccination status and a slightly lower all-cause mortality. Correlation does not establish cause. There are no good data for revaccination with PPSV23 after five years in the dialysis population. Older texts stated the opposite. The review’s authors still conclude that, until better evidence exists, every patient with chronic kidney disease should be vaccinated, and early. The patient is owed the difference between the sentence that the vaccine is reasonable at low risk, and the sentence that a reduction in mortality has been shown.</p>
<p>After a kidney transplant, live vaccines are contraindicated. Pneumococcal vaccines are not among them. The whole vaccination strategy, including live vaccines, is therefore closed before placement on the waiting list. The record needs the date, the product, and the batch. Without them, a year later it is impossible to decide whether the next dose is missing or would be extra.</p>
<p>The largest reserve is that vaccination never starts. Checking the vaccination history in predialysis care costs less than refining an interval that is used only for the person who actually reaches a vaccine.</p>
<p><em>This is a commentary on two texts, one for clinicians and one for patients, not a treatment decision for an individual patient, and not a substitute for the ACIP recommendations, a summary of product characteristics, or Decree No. 585/2008 Coll.</em></p>
